  <!-- Engineering Science Deep Dive -->
  <div class="p-6 md:p-8 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-hairline shadow-sm space-y-4">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-lg md:text-xl font-bold text-ink">{d.scienceTitle}</h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-body mt-0.5">{d.scienceSub}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-body">
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1">Geldart Fluidization & Minimum Velocity (U_mf)</h3>
        <p>
          The fluidization of fine powders in gas streams is governed by the Ergun equation: &Delta;P / H = 150 &middot; ((1-&epsilon;)&sup2; / &epsilon;&sup3;) &middot; (&mu;_g &middot; U_g / d_p&sup2;) + 1.75 &middot; ((1-&epsilon;) / &epsilon;&sup3;) &middot; (&rho;_g &middot; U_g&sup2; / d_p). In Geldart Group A powders (such as dairy powders, d_p &approx; 20&ndash;80 &mu;m, &rho;_p &lt; 1400 kg/m&sup3;), the bed exhibits substantial stable expansion without bubble formation between U_mf and the bubbling onset velocity U_mb. If the fluidization velocity ratio U_g / U_mf drops below 1.5 during spray delivery, upward gas drag fails to overcome liquid bridge surface tension forces, causing the circulating powder bed to collapse into a static cake (wet defluidization).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1">Binary Spray Atomization & Sauter Mean Diameter (d32)</h3>
        <p>
          Droplet size produced by external-mix two-fluid nozzles is modeled via Lefebvre's kinetic shear equation: d32 = A &middot; (&sigma;_L / (&rho;_A &middot; v_R&sup2;))^0.5 &middot; (1 + 1/ALR) + B &middot; (&mu;_L&sup2; / (&sigma;_L &middot; &rho;_L))^0.5 &middot; (1 + 1/ALR), where ALR is the atomization air-to-liquid mass flow ratio and v_R is the relative sonic shear velocity. To prevent overwetting, d32 must remain substantially smaller than the mean particle diameter (d32 &lt; 0.6 &middot; d50).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1">Viscous Stokes Collision Criteria (St_v)</h3>
        <p>
          Agglomeration versus rebound during inter-particle collisions with binder liquid films is evaluated by the dynamic viscous Stokes number: St_v = (2 &middot; m_p &middot; v_0) / (9 &middot; &pi; &middot; &mu;_b &middot; R_b&sup2;). As established by Ennis and Tardos, successful coalescence into porous instantized granules requires St_v &lt; St_v,crit, typically St_v &lt; 0.15. Higher kinetic velocities cause rebound and dry fines elutriation, whereas excessively low Stokes numbers with high moisture lead to catastrophic lump agglomeration.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-ink mb-1">Combustible Dust Deflagration & NFPA 68 Sizing</h3>
        <p>
          Most organic culinary powders are combustible dusts (Class St 1, K_st = 100&ndash;200 bar&middot;m/s, P_max = 7.5&ndash;9.5 bar). Turbulent airflow generates electrostatic charge by particle triboelectric contact: Q = &int; I_tribo dt. The stored capacitive spark energy is E_spark = 0.5 &middot; C &middot; V&sup2;. If equipment grounding resistance exceeds 10 Ohms, accumulated electrostatic potential can discharge with energy exceeding the powder's Minimum Ignition Energy (MIE &lt; 10&ndash;25 mJ), triggering an internal deflagration. NFPA 68 vent sizing requires relief area A_v to ensure reduced deflagration pressure P_red does not exceed vessel yield limits.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Technical Comparison Table -->
